From 0e60b2c4357f20c3326313a18796829f1a33083c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 17:19:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] shell_arith_sink: quoted subscript substitutions in
 `let` / `[[ -eq ]]` operands are sinks
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`let` and `[[ … -eq … ]]` remove their operands' shell quotes, then bash evaluates
the remaining text as arithmetic, and an array subscript in that text runs its
command substitutions. The scanner skipped apostrophe spans in those frames as
data ("let and [[ ]] parse shell words before evaluating operands"), so these
real bash 5.2.21 marker runs were missed on main (t_01a1dace884ce45d, found in
review of PR #2981):

  let 'a[$(bash P)]=1'          let 'x=a[$(bash P)]'
  declare -A h; let 'h[$(bash P)]+=1'
  [[ 1 -eq 'a[$(bash P)]' ]]    [[ 'a[$(bash P)]' -eq 1 ]]

The same hole covered every other way to hide the opener from the shell but
not from the evaluator: `$'…'` (also `\x24(` / `\x5b`), double-quoted `\$(`
and `` \` ``, unquoted `\$\(`, `$\(`, `'$'\(`, quote concatenation
(`'x=a[''$(P)]'`), and `${v:-a[\$(P)]}`.

Fix: `let` / `]]` frames record where their operands start. In such a frame
(subscript and parameter frames inside it belong to the same word), a quoted
span that can supply a substitution opener (`$(`, `$((` — `$((P) )` runs —,
backtick, or a trailing `$`), an escaped opener (`\$(`, `\$\(`, `$\(`,
`` \` ``) or a `"$"` close fails closed when a `[` appears earlier in the
frame. An ANSI-C span with any escape fails closed outright (escapes can spell
both `[` and `$(`). The `[` search resumes past the last miss, so the scan
stays linear. The quick no-substitution exit now also requires no backslash.

Kept as data (real bash runs none of them; regression-tested): the control
`let '${v:-$(P)}'`, `let '$(P)'`, `let 'x=$(( $(P) ))'`,
`[[ 1 -eq 1 && x == '$(P)' ]]`, `[[ 1 -eq '$(P)' && 1 -eq a[1] ]]`,
`let 'a[$i]=1'`, `let 'a[$(date +%s)]=1'`, and quoted text after the frame
ends. No existing data case flips. Known coarse false positives (all fail
closed, none runs in bash): a `[` in an earlier separate `let` argument or
`[[` operand (`let 'a[1]=1' 'b=$(P)'`), `let 'a[1]+$(P)'`, and syntax-error
shapes such as `let a[\$(P)]=1`.

Guard level: scan_gateway_lifecycle now refuses the quoted shapes (padded
payload past the scan cap). It still cannot see the referenced script behind
unquoted backslash-escaped substitutions (t_01a1db0af44d1601) or any backtick
substitution, even `echo \`payload.sh\`` on main (t_01a1db0c38d72914); those
9 guard-level shapes are strict xfail naming their follow-up card.

Proof:
- RED on origin/main be9b942274: new tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_arith_word_quotes.py
  fails 24/24 sink shapes, every one on `substitution_in_arithmetic` (bash
  marker ran each time); 10 data shapes pass.
- GREEN: all 41 test files importing shell_arith_sink / shell_heredoc /
  lifecycle_guard plus the new file: 2824 passed, 0 failed.
- scripts/check --base origin/main: 20 checks ok.
